    "created_at": "2022-03-06T03:30:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-03-06T03:30:09Z",
    "body": "Hello,\r\nI'm interested in contributing to this.\r\n\r\nAs far as I have read:\r\n\r\n- when setting the last activation timestamp for each active `Window` inside *browsingContexts*, current high resolution time is called with the `Window` object itself (6.4.2., 3. of \"activation notification\")\r\n- both sticky activations and transient activations are states of a `Window`\r\n\r\nSo I agree with the previous comments that it makes sense to compare \"the current high resolution time with respect to W (which is the `Window` in question)\" with \"the last activation timestamp\" which is a high resolution time given in respect to W.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nI believe the wording \"current high resolution time with respect to (some window object)\" should explain which global object to pass to current high resolution time. If this issue could be fixed with a documentation fix, I am ready to send a patch/pull request. If any more actions are needed, please guide me as to which actions are needed.\r\n\r\nThank you",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
